    "text_blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Neither SGLT2 inhibitors nor GLP-1 receptor agonists are universally considered the single default \"first-line\" monotherapy for type 2 diabetes; metformin remains the traditional first-line choice . However, current guidelines from the American Diabetes Association (ADA) and European Association for the Study of Diabetes recommend choosing either an SGLT2 inhibitor or a GLP-1 receptor agonist right away (or alongside metformin) depending on a patient's specific organ-protection and weight-loss needs.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "Neither SGLT2 inhibitors nor GLP-1 receptor agonists are universally considered the single default \"first-line\" monotherapy for type 2 diabetes"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Key Comparison"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Glycemic Control (A1c): Both classes lower HbA1c by roughly 0.7% to 1.0%, though GLP-1 receptor agonists often provide a stronger reduction in postprandial (after-meal) blood sugar and overall A1c, while SGLT2 inhibitors excel at lowering fasting blood glucose.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Heart Failure (HF) and Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD): SGLT2 inhibitors are preferred first-line choices for patients with established heart failure (especially HFrEF) or chronic kidney disease because they significantly slow renal decline and reduce hospitalizations for heart failure .",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Atherosclerotic Cardiovascular Disease (ASCVD) and Weight Loss: GLP-1 receptor agonists are preferred when the primary goals are major weight loss (such as with high-dose semaglutide) or reducing major adverse cardiovascular events (strokes and heart attacks) in patients with high ASCVD risk .",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              3,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Administration: SGLT2 inhibitors are taken orally (once daily), whereas GLP-1 receptor agonists are available in either oral or subcutaneous (daily or weekly injection) forms.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              5
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Combination Therapy: They can be safely prescribed together, and guidelines often support dual therapy when patients need additive benefits for blood sugar, blood pressure, and weight.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              6,
              7
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
